Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Half the Melody Is in the Other Room

Story & Context: The debut track begins with László playing an eight-note tárogató melody alone over a nearly empty 132 BPM 2-step beat. On the second pass, notes two, four, six, and eight disappear from his part and Eszter answers them on cimbalom. The contour remains identical, but the attack character alternates between breath and hammer. Amara then removes the kick under every cimbalom note, allowing the hammered attacks to function as part of the rhythmic grid.

Márton enters after sixteen bars with short bowed fifths that occupy the gaps between sub-bass phrases rather than reinforcing them. Nia uses brush drags during two complete snare omissions, making the groove feel continuous even when the electronic backbeat vanishes.

In the middle section, the hocket becomes less symmetrical: László may play three notes before Eszter answers with one, then Eszter may carry two attacks before returning the line. The melody is still one phrase, but ownership changes unpredictably. The final repetition strips away drums completely, leaving tárogató and cimbalom to reconstruct the hook acoustically in the dry switch room.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Two adjacent maintenance rooms divided by a glass partition, tárogató player visible on one side and cimbalom mallets on the other, a paper melody line drawn across both rooms with alternate notes missing from each side. No conventional lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to “your half,” “my half,” “same line,” and “finish it from there.”

Track 2: Dead Stroke Replaces the Clap

Story & Context: Amara programs the EP's most direct garage rhythm: swung hats, short sub notes, syncopated kicks, and a dry clap on two and four. Eszter begins replacing every fourth clap with a palm-muted cimbalom dead-stroke. Because the acoustic hit is slightly earlier and lower in pitch, the backbeat seems to tilt without changing tempo.

László uses only low-register reed fragments, never more than two notes at a time. Nia shadows the missing electronic claps with rim clicks but leaves several gaps completely empty. Márton plays pizzicato bass-viola notes on offbeats where the sub has been removed, creating a alternating synthetic/acoustic bass line that never fully overlaps.

Halfway through, all programmed claps disappear for eight bars. Eszter and Nia rebuild the backbeat using cimbalom dead-strokes and shell taps alone while the kick sequence remains electronic. When the clap returns, it sounds almost too clean, so Amara gradually shortens it until it resembles the acoustic attacks it replaced.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Old telephone punch-card ledger with every fourth backbeat box stamped in dark ink, cimbalom mallet resting across the page, brushed snare and compact drum machine nearby under fluorescent light. Spoken fragments limited to “replace the clap,” “leave one empty,” “wood after wire,” and “shorten it again.”

Track 3: Relay Cabinet Keeps the Last Note

Story & Context: The EP closer uses the slowest-feeling groove despite staying at 130 BPM. The drum pattern is sparse, with long kick gaps and only a few hat clusters. László and Eszter perform a descending hocket phrase that always stops one note before its apparent resolution. Instead of either player completing it, Amara sends the penultimate tárogató tone into a short tape delay. The single repeat lands where the missing final note would have been.

Márton sustains a low bass-viola pedal under the first half while the electronic sub remains absent. Later, the roles reverse: sub-bass holds the pedal and the viola disappears entirely. Nia reduces to brushes on snare rim and soft frame-drum taps.

For the final thirty-two bars, the tape delay feedback is shortened gradually until the phantom last note no longer reaches the expected landing point. László and Eszter continue stopping early, forcing the listener to supply the missing resolution mentally. The track ends with one relay cabinet door clicking shut in the room microphone after all instruments have stopped.

